// Concept texts, batch 5 (names EXHILARATED to HAPHEPHOBE).
// Row: [name, group code, short, feels, looks, misreading, help]. An empty help text uses the group's default.
// Group codes: P phobia, A anxiety, M mood, D disorder, T thinking pattern, C control or manipulation tactic,
// R trait or relationship pattern, V harmful habit, U strength or virtue, K practice or tool,
// F fascination or urge, B body or medical.
export default [
  ["EXHILARATED", "M", "A bright, high-energy feeling of joy, excitement or being fully alive.", "A rush of energy and joy that can make the body feel light and the mind wide open. It often fades after a while, and that is normal.", "Laughing easily, talking fast, moving with energy, and wanting to share the moment with others.", "Being exhilarated is not the same as being manic. It is a concern when the high lasts for days with little sleep or leads to risky choices.", ""],
  ["EXPLODING HEAD SYNDROME", "B", "A rare sleep-related experience where a sudden loud bang seems to go off inside the head.", "The sound is sudden and frightening, though it causes no pain. It usually happens as you fall asleep or wake, and it can leave the heart pounding.", "A brief jolt, an imagined loud bang or flash of light, and a racing heartbeat right after waking or dozing off.", "It is not a sign of a brain explosion or a serious injury. It is still worth seeing a doctor to rule out other causes, especially if it happens often.", "See a doctor about this, since it is worth checking. If it is affecting your mood or safety, call or text 988 in the US. In an emergency, call 911."],
  ["EXPLOITATIVE", "R", "A person who takes unfair advantage of others to get what they want, with little concern for the cost.", "Being around them can feel like being used. You may find yourself giving more time, money or effort than you agreed to, and feeling guilty when you try to stop.", "Favors that keep growing, using your kindness to dodge obligations, and blaming you when you say no.", "Asking for help or wanting something is not exploitation. The pattern is harmful when one person's needs are always met at the other's expense.", ""],
  ["EXPRESSIVE", "U", "Able to share feelings, ideas and creativity openly through words, art, movement or everyday warmth.", "Expressing yourself can feel freeing and bright. It can also feel exposed at first, especially when what you share is tender or unfinished.", "Telling people how you feel, sharing a story with energy, drawing or writing without holding back, and showing care through gestures.", "Being expressive is not the same as oversharing or having no boundaries. It is healthy when it fits the setting and leaves room for others to speak.", ""],
  ["EYE TWITCHING (MYOKYMIA)", "B", "A small, repeated flutter of an eyelid muscle that is usually harmless and often linked to stress or tiredness.", "The eyelid flickers or jumps, often without pain. It can be annoying or worrying, especially when it lasts for days or seems to spread.", "A quick, rhythmic twitch in the lower or upper lid, with tired eyes and tension in the face.", "A twitch is usually not a sign of serious disease. It is still worth a doctor's visit if it lasts for weeks, spreads, or comes with drooping or swelling.", "See a doctor if the twitch lasts for weeks or spreads to other parts of the face. If it is affecting your mood or safety, call or text 988 in the US. In an emergency, call 911."],
  ["FACTITIOUS", "R", "Artificial or made up, not genuine, as when feelings, illness or a story is presented as real when it is not.", "Being around someone factitious can feel confusing and unsettling. You may sense that something is not quite real, and doubt your own read on the situation.", "Dramatic claims that do not match the facts, emotional displays that seem staged, and stories that change when checked.", "Everyone exaggerates sometimes, and not every story is a lie. The pattern matters when false presentation is used to gain attention, care or control.", ""],
  ["FACTITIOUS DISORDER", "D", "A mental health condition in which a person deliberately makes up or causes illness symptoms in self or others.", "The person may feel a deep need for care, attention or a sick role, and may feel ashamed or trapped by the pattern. Those around them often feel confused and worried.", "Repeated medical visits, detailed symptoms that do not fit test results, and hospital stays that follow the same pattern.", "It is a real condition that usually comes from deep distress, not a simple choice to deceive. It is harmful when symptoms are caused on purpose, because real medical harm can follow.", "A doctor or therapist who knows this condition can help. If you have thoughts of ending your life or hurting yourself, call or text 988 in the US now, and call 911 if you are in danger."],
  ["FALSE ACCUSATION", "C", "Blaming someone for something they did not do, to shift attention, gain control or avoid responsibility.", "The person being accused may feel shocked, ashamed and forced to defend every word. Self-doubt can grow, even when they know the claim is not true.", "Sudden claims that you said or did something you did not, especially in front of others, and refusal to discuss evidence.", "Disagreeing or raising a concern is not a false accusation. It is harmful when a claim is invented or twisted to punish someone or to win a conflict.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["FALSE ALLIANCE", "C", "Pretending to be on someone's side in order to gain their trust, then using that trust for another purpose.", "It can feel like finally having a true ally, which makes the later betrayal sharper. You may share things you later wish you had kept private.", "Quick promises of loyalty, taking your side in private but not in public, and quietly passing on what you said.", "Forming a real alliance or working together is healthy. The pattern is harmful when loyalty is only a tool to gain information or control.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["FALSE APOLOGIZER", "R", "A person who says sorry often but does not change the behavior, so the apology ends the conversation without repair.", "Being around them can feel like being pulled back into the same argument. The apology sounds sincere, yet the hurt returns soon after.", "Quick 'sorry, but' statements, apologies that shift blame back to you, and the same issue coming back again and again.", "Apologizing is good and is not always a trick. The pattern matters when apologies replace real change and keep the other person from being heard.", ""],
  ["FALSE EQUIVALENCE", "T", "Treating two unlike things as if they were the same, which can hide real differences and make choices feel harder.", "It can feel frustrating and confusing when a real difference is waved away. You may feel dismissed or unsure whether your concern matters.", "Phrases like 'everyone does it' or 'it is all the same' used to end a discussion about a clear difference in harm or cost.", "Comparing two things is not always false. It is a problem when the comparison ignores important differences that change the meaning.", ""],
  ["FALSE INTEGRATION", "T", "Believing an experience or pain has been fully processed when it has only been pushed out of view.", "There can be relief at feeling done with something, followed by old feelings returning without warning. It can feel like a quiet tug of unfinished business.", "Saying 'I am over it' quickly, avoiding the topic, or moving straight to a new goal without any reflection.", "Healing takes time, and moving on is healthy. It is a problem when the feeling is only hidden, so it returns in other forms like anger or numbness.", ""],
  ["FALSE MIRRORING", "C", "Copying someone's words, tone, interests or feelings to build a fast, false sense of closeness and trust.", "It can feel like finally being truly understood. Over time, you may notice the closeness was built on imitation rather than real knowing.", "Repeating your phrases back, claiming the same tastes or hurts right away, and matching every mood you show.", "Finding common ground and feeling understood is normal. It becomes harmful when the mirroring is used to lower your guard or shape what you think.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["FALSE SELF", "R", "A polished image shown to others while the real feelings and needs stay hidden.", "Living this way can feel lonely, tiring and empty, even when others praise you. The gap between the outside and the inside can be hard to bear.", "Always being agreeable in public, rarely showing real opinions, and seeming unsure of what you actually want.", "Adapting to social settings is normal and can be kind. It becomes a concern when the mask stays on so long that the true self feels unknown.", ""],
  ["FALSE SURRENDER", "C", "Appearing to give up or agree, then quietly continuing the same conflict or control later.", "It can feel like the argument is finally over, so you relax your guard. Later, the old pressure returns, which can leave you confused and worn out.", "Saying 'fine, you win' while still acting the same way, or agreeing quickly and then bringing the issue back in a new form.", "Changing your mind after honest thought is healthy. The pattern is harmful when agreement is only a tactic to lower your guard and gain control later.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["FATAL FAMILIAL INSOMNIA", "B", "A very rare inherited illness that steadily destroys the ability to sleep, with serious changes in body and mind.", "Sleep becomes harder and harder to reach, leaving the body exhausted and the mind foggy. The changes can be frightening for both the person and their family.", "Worsening insomnia that does not respond to rest, confusion, sweating, changes in movement, and a steady decline over months.", "It is not a common cause of trouble sleeping, and most sleep problems have other causes. Long-term sleep loss with confusion or movement changes needs a doctor's review.", "See a doctor right away if sleep loss is severe or getting worse. If it is affecting your mood or safety, call or text 988 in the US. In an emergency, call 911."],
  ["FATIGUE", "B", "A deep tiredness that does not fully ease with rest and can affect the body, mood and focus.", "The body feels heavy and slow, and even small tasks can seem hard. Motivation may dip, and the mind can feel foggy by afternoon.", "Yawning often, slow movement, needing long naps, trouble focusing, and irritability when tasks pile up.", "Being tired after a busy week is normal. Fatigue that lasts for weeks, or comes with weight change, fever or low mood, needs a doctor's check.", "See a doctor if tiredness lasts for weeks or affects daily life. If it is affecting your mood or safety, call or text 988 in the US. In an emergency, call 911."],
  ["FAWN RESPONSE", "A", "A stress response in which a person tries to calm or please a threat in order to feel safe.", "Under pressure, the body pushes to smooth things over, even when you do not want to. Afterward, you may feel drained, resentful or unsure of yourself.", "Quick agreement, over-apologizing, anticipating others' moods, and a flat tone when someone is upset.", "Being kind or accommodating is not always a fawn response. It is a stress pattern when it happens automatically and you lose track of your own needs.", ""],
  ["FAWNING", "R", "Pleasing others and putting their needs first to avoid conflict, rejection or anger.", "It can feel like walking on eggshells, always watching someone's face. The relief of a calm moment often comes with a quiet sense of losing yourself.", "Agreeing with everything, doing favors before being asked, and hiding disagreement until it turns into resentment.", "Kindness and generosity are strengths. Fawning is harmful when it keeps you from speaking up and the relationship depends on your silence.", ""],
  ["FEAR", "A", "An alarm response to a real or imagined danger that prepares the body to protect itself.", "The body tightens, the heart speeds up, and the mind jumps to what might go wrong. It can be brief and useful, or it can linger and feel overwhelming.", "Startling easily, tense shoulders, avoidance, checking, and restlessness that is hard to explain.", "Fear is not a sign of weakness, and it is not always a sign something is wrong. It becomes a problem when it grows large enough to shape daily choices.", ""],
  ["FEAR OF ABANDONMENT", "A", "An intense worry that important people will leave, reject or stop caring, even without a clear reason.", "It can feel like a sudden drop in the stomach when a message is late or a plan changes. The worry can make it hard to trust that connection is steady.", "Frequent reassurance seeking, checking messages often, clinging, or pulling away first to avoid being left.", "Wanting closeness and worrying about loss is human. It becomes a problem when fear drives every choice and makes it hard to enjoy the relationship.", ""],
  ["FEAR OF FAILURE", "A", "A strong worry about falling short, making mistakes or being judged as not good enough.", "It can feel like pressure in the chest and a dread of being seen trying and not succeeding. The worry may keep you from starting at all.", "Delaying tasks, overpreparing, quitting early, or saying it does not matter after a setback.", "Caring about results is healthy. It is a problem when the fear of a mistake stops you from trying, learning or asking for help.", ""],
  ["FEAR OF INTIMACY", "A", "A worry about being emotionally close to someone, which can lead to holding back or keeping others at a distance.", "Closeness can feel exposing, as if being known might bring hurt or loss of control. You may feel relieved when you pull back, then lonely afterward.", "Keeping conversations light, ending relationships when they deepen, and avoiding talk about feelings or future plans.", "Needing space is normal, and not everyone wants deep closeness at every stage. It is a problem when fear keeps you from relationships you want.", ""],
  ["FEAR OF SUCCESS", "A", "A worry that reaching a goal will bring pressure, envy, loss of closeness or a changed sense of self.", "Progress can feel uneasy, as if moving forward might cost you something. You may hesitate just when things are going well.", "Sabotaging nearly finished work, downplaying wins, or avoiding chances that could bring recognition.", "Worry about change is normal, and not every success is easy to handle. It becomes a problem when fear of being seen as successful keeps you from your goals.", ""],
  ["FEAR OF VULNERABILITY", "A", "A worry about showing weakness, need or emotion, which can make openness feel unsafe.", "Opening up can feel like standing without armor. The urge to protect yourself can be strong, even with people who have earned your trust.", "Changing the subject when feelings come up, using humor to deflect, or appearing fine while struggling.", "Choosing whom to trust is wise. It becomes harmful when the fear of being seen stops you from asking for help you need.", ""],
  ["FEARFUL-AVOIDANT", "R", "A person who both longs for closeness and pulls away from it, often because past hurt makes trust feel risky.", "Being around them can feel like a push and pull, with warmth followed by sudden distance. You may feel unsure where you stand and afraid of getting it wrong.", "Going quiet after intimacy, reaching out then withdrawing, and quietly testing whether you will stay.", "A pattern of wanting and fearing closeness is common after hurt and is not a fixed identity. It is a problem when both people are stuck in cycles of hurt and withdrawal.", ""],
  ["FEARLESS AUTHENTIC FREEDOM", "U", "Living truthfully and acting from your own values, even when fear is present.", "Freedom can feel light and steady, like being yourself without asking permission. It often comes with some nerves, which is a normal part of honest living.", "Speaking your mind kindly, making choices that fit your values, and saying no without a long apology.", "Fearlessness does not mean never feeling afraid or ignoring risk. It means acting with care and honesty while the fear is still there.", ""],
  ["FEARLESS AUTHENTIC SELF", "U", "Being honest about who you are, what you need and what you feel, and letting others see that version of you.", "Being your real self can feel relieving, though it may bring a flutter of worry about how others will react. Over time, it often brings a steadier calm.", "Saying what you think, dropping a role you do not like, and telling people the truth about a plan or a need.", "Being authentic is not an excuse to be unkind or to ignore others' needs. It means telling the truth with respect for the people around you.", ""],
  ["FEARLESS EXPANSION", "U", "Growing into new experiences, ideas or roles with courage, one step at a time.", "Growth can feel exciting and a little uncertain, like opening a door you had kept shut. The excitement often grows as small steps begin to add up.", "Trying something new, asking a question in a group, or accepting an invitation that feels a little outside your comfort zone.", "Growth does not require reckless risks or pushing past your limits. Healthy expansion is measured, with rest and support built in.", ""],
  ["FEARLESS HEART", "U", "Facing hard feelings and difficult moments with courage and compassion, instead of running from them.", "A steady heart can feel warm and grounded, even when the moment is hard. It is not the absence of fear but a calm willingness to stay present.", "Staying in a difficult conversation, naming a feeling aloud, and returning to a hard task after a rough day.", "A fearless heart is not the same as being unfazed or numb. It is harmful when it is used to ignore pain or push through without rest.", ""],
  ["FEARLESS SELF-EXPANSION", "U", "Developing your abilities and understanding of yourself with bravery, curiosity and patience.", "It can feel like a quiet confidence growing from inside. Some moments feel awkward, but each one adds to a broader sense of who you are.", "Trying a new skill, reflecting honestly on mistakes, and asking others for feedback.", "Self-growth does not mean constant self-improvement or never being satisfied with yourself. It is healthy when it includes rest and self-kindness.", ""],
  ["FEIGNED SELF-AWARENESS", "C", "Pretending to understand your faults so that criticism stops, without changing the behavior.", "It can feel like a conversation has been closed too quickly, leaving no real acknowledgment. You may feel frustrated, unheard and unsure whether to push harder.", "Saying 'I know I am the problem' and then repeating the same behavior, or deflecting any detail with a quick admission.", "Honest self-reflection is healthy and worth valuing. It is harmful when the admission is a shield used to avoid real accountability.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["FETISHISM", "F", "A strong sexual interest in a specific object, body part or material, beyond what is typical.", "The interest can feel strong and sometimes hard to explain, and it may bring shame or secrecy. Many people feel relief when they understand it more clearly.", "A repeated focus on a particular item or body part, with strong interest that shapes sexual thoughts or fantasies.", "Having an interest is not the same as acting on it. Acting without consent is harm, and an interest never gives anyone the right to act on another person.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["FIBRODYSPLASIA OSSIFICANS PROGRESSIVA", "B", "A very rare inherited condition where muscle and connective tissue slowly turn into bone outside the skeleton.", "Movement can become stiff and painful as new bone forms. Flare-ups can be sudden and frightening, and daily tasks may take more effort over time.", "Hard lumps or swelling under the skin, stiff joints, limited movement, and pain after bumps, falls or injections.", "It is not caused by lifestyle, and it is not ordinary muscle soreness. It needs care from doctors who know the condition, and early specialist care matters.", "See a doctor who knows this rare condition, and act quickly on new swelling or pain. If it is affecting your mood or safety, call or text 988 in the US. In an emergency, call 911."],
  ["FIGHT RESPONSE", "A", "A stress reaction in which the body gets ready to confront a threat with force, speed and tension.", "The chest tightens, the jaw sets, and anger or urgency can rise quickly. Once the threat passes, the body may stay wound up for a while.", "A raised voice, clenched fists, interrupting, or a sharp tone when someone feels threatened.", "Anger is not always harmful, and standing up for yourself is healthy. It becomes a problem when every disagreement triggers a fight reaction.", ""],
  ["FIGHT-OR-FLIGHT ACTIVATION", "A", "The body's built-in alarm system, which speeds the heart and readies the muscles to act in danger.", "The heart pounds, breath gets quick, and the mind feels sharp and alert. Hands may shake, and rest can feel impossible until the alarm settles.", "Rapid breathing, sweating, restlessness, quick reactions, and a sense of urgency out of proportion to the situation.", "This reaction is normal and protective, not a sign that you are broken. It is a problem when it keeps firing in safe situations and disturbs sleep or daily life.", ""],
  ["FINANCIAL ABUSER", "R", "A person who uses money to control, punish or limit another person's choices and independence.", "Being around them can feel like walking a tightrope, afraid of how a purchase or request will be received. You may feel guilty, watched and unable to plan for yourself.", "Controlling access to bank accounts, demanding receipts, punishing spending, and keeping you from working or saving.", "Sharing finances or making joint decisions is not abuse by itself. It is abuse when money is used to force compliance or to isolate someone.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["FINANCIAL CONTROL", "C", "Limiting another person's access to money, bank accounts or decisions about spending.", "It can feel like having no say over your own money, with a constant worry about what will be allowed. Small purchases may feel like a negotiation.", "Holding all the cards, asking for every purchase, cutting off access, and handing out an allowance without discussion.", "Managing a shared budget together is healthy, and not every money rule is control. It is harmful when one person removes choice and punishes the other for spending.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["FINANCIAL MANIPULATION", "C", "Using money, debts, gifts or promises about finances to pressure someone into choices that serve the other person.", "It can feel like being cornered, with guilt or fear pushing you to pay or agree. Later, you may feel tricked or unsure what was really owed.", "Gifts with strings attached, threats about money, guilt about spending, and promises of future support that never arrive.", "Talking about money honestly and asking for help is normal. It is harmful when money is used to pressure, confuse or punish someone.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["FLAGELLOMANIAC", "F", "A sexual interest in being whipped or flogged, or in whipping another person, as an intense erotic focus.", "The interest can feel strong, exciting and sometimes confusing, especially when it does not fit how you see yourself. It can bring shame or secrecy that weighs on daily life.", "A repeated focus on whipping or flogging in sexual thoughts, searches or fantasies, often with a strong pull to act it out.", "Having an interest is not the same as acting on it. Acting without consent is harm, and any activity should involve a willing adult who has clearly agreed.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["FLAME", "F", "A romantic fixation on someone who feels like a spark, often a past or unreachable person, that keeps flaring back.", "The pull can feel exciting, painful and hard to put down, often returning when life feels quiet. Each flare can feel like hope, followed by a sharp drop.", "Thinking about the person often, checking their profile, replaying old moments, and feeling thrown off by any news.", "Missing someone or remembering a meaningful bond is normal. It becomes a problem when the fixation blocks new connection or keeps you stuck in hope with no contact.", ""],
  ["FLASHBACK INTEGRATION", "K", "A practice of gently linking a flashback to the present, so the memory feels like the past rather than now.", "Practicing it can feel shaky at first, with memories feeling close. Over time, many people feel more able to stay in the present while still remembering.", "Noticing a flashback starting, naming what you see and hear, and reminding yourself of where you are now.", "This is not forcing yourself to relive trauma or pretending it did not happen. Integration works best with support and paced steps, not pushing through.", ""],
  ["FLASHBACKS", "A", "Sudden, vivid returns of a past experience that can feel as if it is happening again in the present.", "The body may react as if danger is here, with a racing heart, shaking or a sense of being somewhere else. It can be frightening and leave you drained afterward.", "A sudden startle, staring into space, reliving sounds or smells, or avoiding places linked to a past event.", "Flashbacks are a common response to overwhelming experiences, not a sign of weakness or danger. They are worth treating with a trauma-informed clinician when they are frequent.", ""],
  ["FLAT", "M", "A dull, muted feeling with little energy, color or pleasure in things that usually matter.", "Emotions can feel dim, as if someone turned down the brightness on life. Even good news may land with little response, which can be frustrating.", "Little change in facial expression or tone, slow replies, and fewer interests or plans than before.", "Feeling flat for a while is common after stress, illness or loss. It is worth talking with a doctor or counselor when it lasts for weeks or affects daily life.", ""],
  ["FLIGHT RESPONSE", "A", "The body's urge to run away from danger, driven by a fast burst of stress hormones.", "The legs want to move and the mind races toward the exit. Escape can feel like the only option, even when the threat is small or uncertain.", "Sudden restlessness, pacing, leaving a room abruptly, or avoiding a conversation before it starts.", "A flight response is not cowardice. It is an old survival reflex, and it can fire at a memory or a loud noise as easily as at real danger.", ""],
  ["FLOURISHING INTEGRATION", "U", "Bringing your many parts into a life that feels whole, purposeful and growing.", "Life can feel more connected, as if work, relationships and values fit together. Energy returns slowly, and each part of you has a place.", "Keeping promises to yourself, pursuing a few meaningful goals, and noticing growth in how you handle hard days.", "Flourishing is not constant happiness or perfect balance. It grows in ordinary seasons, including rest, grief and imperfect weeks.", ""],
  ["FLUID EXPRESSION", "U", "Sharing feelings and ideas in a natural, flexible way that fits the moment.", "Words come more easily, and you can move between serious and playful without strain. It feels relaxed, as though you are letting something flow.", "Speaking openly in conversation, changing tone to match the setting, and letting a creative project take its own shape.", "Fluid expression is not oversharing or saying everything you think. It means being honest and adaptable, while still choosing what to share.", ""],
  ["FLUSHING", "B", "A sudden warm redness in the face, neck or chest, often linked to emotion or heat.", "Warmth rises quickly in the face and neck, and the skin may tingle. Embarrassment or stress can make it feel more noticeable than it is.", "Red patches on the face, neck or chest that come and go, sometimes with sweating or a racing heartbeat.", "Flushing does not always mean you are lying or anxious. Many people flush from heat, spicy food, medicine or simple changes in circulation.", ""],
  ["FLYING MONKEY", "R", "A person who carries out harmful tasks for someone else, such as spying or delivering messages.", "You may get messages or pressure from someone who is not directly speaking to you. It can feel confusing, guilty, and hard to know who to trust.", "Relaying hostile messages, reporting on your whereabouts, or pressing you to drop a boundary on someone else's behalf.", "Someone who passes on a message is not always a manipulator themselves. It is harmful when the role is used to pressure, isolate or control.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["FOMO", "F", "The worry of missing out on experiences, news or connections that others seem to enjoy.", "A tight, restless feeling appears when you see others together or online. It can pull your attention away from what you are doing right now.", "Checking social feeds often, saying yes to plans out of pressure, or feeling upset when you cannot join in.", "Wanting to be included is normal and human. FOMO becomes a problem when it drives constant checking, overspending or exhaustion.", ""],
  ["FOREIGN ACCENT SYNDROME", "B", "A rare condition where speech suddenly sounds like a different accent, usually after a brain injury or stroke.", "Speech may come out in a way that feels unfamiliar and strange to the speaker. Frustration and embarrassment can be strong, especially when others react to it.", "A changed accent or rhythm of speech that others notice, often after a head injury, stroke or other neurological event.", "It is not an act or a choice, and the person is not faking it. It is rare, and it needs a medical check to find the cause.", "A sudden change in speech needs a doctor right away. If it is affecting your mood or safety, call or text 988 in the US. In an emergency, call 911."],
  ["FORGIVENESS", "U", "Choosing to let go of resentment toward someone who hurt you, without excusing the harm.", "Forgiveness can bring a sense of lightness after carrying anger for a long time. It can also feel tender, because old hurt may return before it settles.", "Naming a hurt honestly, deciding not to keep punishing the person, and still setting limits where they are needed.", "Forgiving is not the same as forgetting, reconciling, or saying the harm was fine. You can forgive and still keep a safe distance.", ""],
  ["FORGIVING LIBERATION", "U", "The freedom that can come from releasing resentment and self-blame over past hurts.", "Setting down a long-held grudge can feel like a weight lifting off your chest. Some days the relief is clear, and other days old anger still visits.", "Speaking about the past with less heat, sleeping more easily, and feeling less drawn to replay old arguments.", "Liberation does not mean the hurt never mattered. It means the past no longer controls your daily choices, and that can take a long time.", ""],
  ["FORMICOPHILIA", "F", "A sexual interest in the feeling of insects or small creatures crawling on the body.", "The sensation of crawling can feel intense and hard to ignore, and the interest may come back often. It can bring shame, secrecy or confusion.", "Frequent thoughts or searches about insects on the skin, or spending time in ways that seek out that feeling.", "Having an interest is not the same as acting on it. Acting without consent, or causing harm to others or animals, is harmful and is never acceptable.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["FORTITUDE", "U", "Inner strength to keep going through pain, fear or hardship without giving up your values.", "Fortitude can feel like a steady core that holds even when your energy is low. It is quiet more often than dramatic, and it can be tiring.", "Showing up for a hard appointment, sticking with a difficult task, or staying calm while facing a stressful situation.", "Fortitude is not never feeling afraid or pretending to be fine. It is acting with care while the fear is still present.", ""],
  ["FRAGILE X SYNDROME", "B", "A genetic condition that can affect learning, behavior and development, and needs medical care.", "Learning or focusing may feel harder, and everyday tasks can take more effort. Families often feel worry and a need for steady support.", "Delayed speech or language, shyness or anxiety in social settings, and attention or sensory differences.", "It is a real genetic condition, not a sign of poor effort or bad parenting. It is rare in its full form and needs a doctor to confirm it.", "Fragile X needs a doctor and genetic testing to confirm. If it is affecting your mood or safety, call or text 988 in the US. In an emergency, call 911."],
  ["FRAGMENTED IDENTITY", "M", "A feeling that your sense of self is split into pieces that do not fit together.", "You may feel like a different person in different settings, with no clear center. It can be unsettling and lonely, especially when your values seem to shift.", "Strong shifts in opinions, roles or goals across situations, and uncertainty about what you really want.", "Having several sides is normal, and people change over time. It becomes distressing when the gaps cause confusion, shame or constant doubt.", ""],
  ["FREEZE RESPONSE", "A", "A sudden stillness or numbness when the body senses danger and cannot run or fight.", "Movement and speech can seem to stop, and the mind may go blank. Afterward, many people feel ashamed for not acting, which adds pain.", "Going silent, staying still, feeling stuck during a stressful moment, or dissociating while others are talking.", "Freezing is not a failure or a sign of weakness. It is an automatic survival reflex, and it is common after frightening events.", ""],
  ["FREGOLI DELUSION", "D", "A rare belief that different people are secretly the same person in disguise.", "The person may feel watched or followed by familiar strangers. Trust can break down, and the belief can feel very real and frightening.", "Insisting that strangers are a known person in disguise, or treating unrelated people as part of a plot.", "This is a rare condition, not a sign of simple paranoia or lying. It usually signals a brain or mental health condition that needs professional care.", ""],
  ["FRONTOTEMPORAL DEMENTIA", "D", "A type of dementia that mainly changes behavior, personality and language, often early in life.", "Family members may feel confused, hurt, or worried by sudden changes in personality. The person may not realize what is changing in them.", "Loss of social filters, poor judgment, withdrawal, or trouble finding words, while memory may stay relatively intact at first.", "Behavior changes from this condition are not deliberate rudeness or laziness. It is a progressive brain disease, and a neurologist can help.", ""],
  ["FROTTEURISM", "F", "A sexual interest in rubbing against another person's body, often in crowded places.", "The urge can feel strong and sudden in crowds or close spaces. Afterward, many people feel shame, fear of being discovered, or distress about their thoughts.", "Seeking out crowded places in order to press against others, or staying close to strangers without their clear consent.", "Having this interest is not the same as acting on it. Touching anyone without clear consent is harm, and it is never acceptable.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["FULFILLED", "M", "A calm sense that your life has meaning, and that your needs and goals are being met.", "A quiet warmth settles in, with a sense that things are enough as they are. Fulfillment often comes in brief moments rather than lasting forever.", "Taking satisfaction in work, rest or relationships, and feeling willing to share what you have with others.", "Feeling fulfilled does not mean you have no problems or need to feel happy every day. It is a steady sense of purpose that can coexist with stress.", ""],
  ["FULL SELF INTEGRATION", "U", "Bringing together all parts of yourself, including strengths and hard feelings, into one whole.", "You may feel less divided, with fewer gaps between how you act and what you believe. It can bring relief and a grounded sense of who you are.", "Being honest about both good and difficult traits, speaking with consistency, and making choices that reflect your values.", "Integration is not becoming perfect or hiding flaws. It means accepting the whole of who you are, and it grows gradually over time.", ""],
  ["FULL SELF-ACCEPTANCE", "U", "Accepting yourself as you are, with your flaws and strengths, without constant self-judgment.", "Self-criticism quiets down, and you can notice mistakes without feeling worthless. It may feel tender at first, like returning home after a long trip.", "Speaking kindly about your body or mistakes, and allowing yourself to rest without guilt.", "Accepting yourself does not mean giving up on growth or excusing harm you cause. It means relating to yourself with honesty and respect.", ""],
  ["FUTURE FAKER", "R", "A person who makes promises about the future that they never intend to keep.", "You may feel hopeful at first, then slowly confused as plans never arrive. Trust can wear thin, and waiting can feel like a hook you cannot let go of.", "Big promises of a shared home, a trip or a marriage, followed by delays, new excuses and vague talk when pressed.", "Everyone makes plans that change. It is harmful when promises are used to win loyalty, money or time while nothing is ever meant to happen.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["FUTURE FAKING", "C", "Making false promises about a shared future to keep someone close or under control.", "The person feels hopeful and invested, and may stop protecting themselves. Later, the broken promises can leave deep disappointment and self-doubt.", "Talk of marriage, moving in or big plans early on, with dates that keep shifting or never arrive.", "Talking about the future is normal in healthy relationships. It is manipulation when the promises are used to hold someone in place.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["FUTURE ORIENTED HOPE", "U", "A steady belief that tomorrow can bring good things, and that your choices matter.", "A light sense of possibility can enter the day, even when the present is hard. It often grows slowly, through small wins and honest effort.", "Making plans for next month, saying you look forward to something, and taking one step toward a future goal.", "Hope is not denial that problems exist. It is a way of holding difficult facts while still believing that change is possible.", ""],
  ["FUTURE SELF ANCHORING", "K", "A practice of picturing your future self to guide choices made in the present.", "Imagining your future self can feel hopeful, and sometimes a little strange. It can ease the pull of short-term urges by bringing longer goals into view.", "Asking what your future self would thank you for, or writing a note to yourself about a decision.", "This practice is not a way to judge your present self or pressure yourself into perfection. It is a gentle reminder that today's choices shape later days.", ""],
  ["GAG REFLEX", "B", "A reflex that causes a retching or gagging response, often triggered by touch, taste or smell.", "A sudden tightening in the throat and stomach can make swallowing or eating feel difficult. The urge can be embarrassing and hard to control.", "Gagging during dental care, eating certain foods, or feeling sick when something touches the back of the throat.", "A strong gag reflex is common and is not a sign of weakness or a serious illness on its own. It can become a problem if it affects eating or breathing.", ""],
  ["GAMBLING DISORDER", "D", "A condition where the urge to gamble keeps growing despite harm to money, work or relationships.", "The urge can feel urgent, with hope that the next bet will fix losses. Shame and secrecy often grow alongside the debt and stress.", "Chasing losses, hiding bank activity, borrowing to bet, or lying about how much time or money has been spent.", "This is not a character flaw or a lack of willpower. It is a recognized condition, and it responds well to treatment and support.", ""],
  ["GASLIGHTING", "C", "Making someone doubt their own memory, perception or sanity to gain control over them.", "You may feel confused about what really happened, and start second-guessing your memory. Anxiety grows as you try to prove what you know.", "Denying events that happened, saying 'you are too sensitive', or insisting you imagined conversations you clearly remember.", "Disagreeing about facts or memories is normal and can be healthy. Gaslighting is harmful when it is used to make someone distrust their own mind.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["GASLIGHT SHADOW", "T", "The inner habit of doubting your own feelings and memories, often learned from being gaslit.", "You may second-guess your reactions before you have even finished having them. The inner voice can sound harsh and certain, even when it is unsure.", "Saying 'I probably overreacted' after every difficult moment, or apologizing for things that were not your fault.", "Doubt is not always a sign that you are wrong. It becomes harmful when this inner voice keeps you from trusting what you see and feel.", ""],
  ["GASLIGHTER", "R", "A person who repeatedly makes others doubt their own memory, feelings or perception.", "Being around them can leave you uncertain, anxious and quick to apologize. You may find yourself checking your memory before you speak.", "Denying what was said, reframing events as your mistake, and saying 'that never happened' even with evidence.", "Someone can deny a memory honestly and still be wrong. It is harmful when the denial is repeated to make you distrust yourself.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["GASLIGHTOPHILE", "F", "A person drawn to the power of making others doubt their own reality.", "The person may feel a strong pull toward control over another's thinking. Those around them may feel confused or watched, and unsure of what is real.", "Enjoying the confusion they cause, repeatedly testing how far someone will accept a false account, or bragging about it.", "Having a pull toward control is not the same as acting on it. Using these tactics on someone is harmful, and it is worth seeking help to change it.", "If you notice this pull in yourself, a therapist can help you understand it and change how you treat people. If someone is doing this to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan, and the National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["GELOMANIAC", "F", "A person with an intense, persistent pull toward laughter, or toward making others laugh.", "Laughter can feel like an urge that builds up and must be released. It may be exciting, but it can also feel hard to stop once it starts.", "Seeking out comedy constantly, laughing at inappropriate moments, or steering conversations toward jokes to get a reaction.", "Enjoying humor is healthy and very common. It becomes a concern when it crowds out other needs, or is used to hurt or belittle others.", ""],
  ["GENEROUS", "U", "Giving time, care, money or attention freely, with an open heart toward others.", "Giving can feel warm and meaningful, with a sense of connection. It can also feel tiring when you give more than you have, or give without being asked.", "Sharing food or tools, offering help without expecting anything back, and remembering what matters to others.", "Generosity is not the same as saying yes to everything. It is healthiest when it comes from choice and includes care for yourself.", ""],
  ["GENTLE DISCIPLINE", "U", "Keeping steady habits and boundaries through patience and self-respect rather than harshness.", "Structure can feel calm and reassuring, with less pressure to be perfect. Progress comes slowly, and you may notice small wins that build confidence.", "Sticking to a routine with kindness when you slip, and returning to a task without shame.", "Gentle discipline does not mean having no rules or never failing. It is a steady way of keeping commitments while treating yourself with care.", ""],
  ["GENTLE EMOTIONAL REGULATION", "K", "A practice of noticing strong feelings and calming them with kind, steady actions.", "Strong emotions may still rise, but they can feel less overwhelming. Over time, the body can settle more quickly after a hard moment.", "Pausing to name a feeling, using slow breath or a calm place, and choosing a response that fits the situation.", "This practice does not mean suppressing feelings or pretending to be calm. It means making room for them so they can move through you.", ""],
  ["GENTLE EMOTIONAL RELEASE", "K", "A practice of letting feelings move out of the body through tears, movement, writing or sound.", "Release can bring tears, shaking or a sense of relief after holding something in for a long time. It may feel vulnerable, so go at your own pace.", "Crying during a quiet walk, writing an unsent letter, or stretching slowly until the body softens.", "Emotional release is not the same as venting at others or losing control. It is a safe, private way to let feelings out, and it works best with rest afterward.", ""],
  ["GENTLE EMPOWERMENT", "U", "Building confidence to make your own choices, using kindness and steady encouragement.", "A quiet sense of agency can grow, as if you have more say over your life. It may feel unfamiliar at first, especially if you were often told what to do.", "Making a decision you have been postponing, speaking up in a meeting, or asking for what you need.", "Empowerment is not the same as doing everything alone or never needing others. It means choosing with your own judgment and support.", ""],
  ["GENTLE INNER APPROVAL", "U", "Offering yourself a kind, honest sense of acceptance rather than waiting for others' praise.", "You may notice a softer voice inside that says you did enough. The approval does not need to be earned, and it can feel calming.", "Acknowledging your effort after a task, and speaking to yourself as you would to a good friend.", "Inner approval is not self-flattery or ignoring real mistakes. It is a steady respect for yourself that can still hold accountability.", ""],
  ["GENTLE INNER GUIDANCE", "U", "Trusting a calm, steady inner voice to help you choose what matters most.", "A quiet knowing can rise when your mind is still, often as a simple nudge. It may be faint, so it can be easy to drown out with noise.", "Pausing before a decision, noticing a sense of ease or unease in the body, and choosing with patience.", "Inner guidance is not the same as acting on every impulse. It is best checked against facts, values and the advice of people you trust.", ""],
  ["GENTLE INNER LEADER", "U", "Being a steady, caring presence for yourself when making choices and facing difficulty.", "You may feel more grounded, able to hold your own direction when things get loud. It can take time, and some days it feels stronger than others.", "Setting a clear goal for the week, speaking to yourself with firmness and care, and following through on small commitments.", "A gentle inner leader is not harsh, controlling or always certain. It guides with warmth, and it can change course when new facts appear.", ""],
  ["GENTLE INNER PEACE", "U", "A calm, settled feeling inside that stays steady even when life around you is busy.", "Peace can feel like a slow exhale, with the body softening and thoughts less crowded. It often arrives in small moments rather than all at once.", "Speaking more slowly, taking breaks without guilt, and returning to calm after a stressful event.", "Inner peace does not mean never feeling anger, sadness or fear. It means being able to hold those feelings without being overtaken by them.", ""],
  ["GENTLE SELF-FORGIVENESS", "U", "Letting go of harsh self-blame after a mistake, while still taking responsibility for it.", "Shame can loosen its grip, and you may feel room to start again. It can still sting at first, especially when the mistake affected someone you love.", "Naming a mistake plainly, apologizing where it fits, and choosing a calmer way to move forward.", "Self-forgiveness is not excusing harm or avoiding repair. It is what makes repair possible, because self-criticism often freezes people in place.", ""],
  ["GENTLE SELF-PARENTING", "K", "A practice of caring for yourself the way a steady, loving parent would care for a child.", "Speaking to yourself with tenderness may feel awkward at first, especially if you did not receive much care growing up. Over time, it can feel steadying.", "Offering yourself comfort when you are upset, setting bedtimes, and naming your needs out loud.", "Self-parenting is not treating yourself like a child or excusing every choice. It means meeting your needs with both warmth and sensible limits.", ""],
  ["GENTLE SELF-PATIENCE", "U", "Allowing yourself time to grow, heal and learn without rushing or harsh deadlines.", "Pressure can ease, and the weight of expecting instant results may lift. Patience can still feel uncomfortable when you are eager to be finished.", "Accepting slow progress without self-criticism, and resting when a goal takes longer than planned.", "Patience is not the same as giving up or never trying. It means keeping effort steady while accepting the pace that real change needs.", ""],
  ["GENTLE WALKING MEDITATION", "K", "A slow walking practice where you pay close attention to each step, breath and sound around you.", "Walking at a slow pace can settle a busy mind. Attention moves from worries to the feel of the ground under your feet.", "Slow steps on a quiet path, with eyes soft and attention on the body. Pauses may happen now and then to notice the air or the trees.", "It is not the same as a hurried walk for exercise, and it does not need to be silent or perfect. It helps when you return to the steps again and again.", ""],
  ["GEPHYROPHOBE", "P", "A strong fear of crossing bridges, often because of worries about falling, collapse or being high up.", "Approaching a bridge can bring a tight chest, shaky legs and a strong urge to turn back. The fear can feel out of proportion, even when the bridge is safe.", "Avoiding bridges even when a route is much longer, taking a long detour, or gripping the seat and closing the eyes while crossing.", "Fear of bridges is not foolishness or a sign of being weak. It becomes a problem when it narrows where you can go or how you travel.", ""],
  ["GERMOPHOBIA", "P", "A strong, persistent fear of germs, dirt and infection that can lead to constant washing or avoidance.", "Germs can seem everywhere, and touching shared surfaces may bring a rush of dread. Relief from washing is short, and the worry often returns soon after.", "Washing hands many times, avoiding public places or shared objects, and asking others to clean before you can relax.", "Caring about hygiene is healthy. The fear is harmful when it takes up hours each day or stops you from eating, working or seeing people.", ""],
  ["GERONTOPHILIA", "F", "A sexual attraction to older adults. It is a type of interest, not a choice to act on it.", "The attraction can feel real and sometimes confusing, especially if it clashes with what you expect of yourself. Many people feel shame or worry about it.", "Interest in older people may show up as a steady pull in fantasies or dating preferences. It may be private and not seen by others at all.", "Having an interest is not the same as acting on it. Acting on any sexual interest without consent, especially with someone who cannot agree, is harm.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["GHOSTER", "R", "A person who ends contact suddenly, without any explanation, and stops replying to messages.", "Being ghosted can feel confusing and painful. Your mind may replay messages, search for a mistake, and wonder what went wrong.", "Replies that stop mid-conversation, plans cancelled without a word, and silence after a close connection that seemed warm.", "Ghosting is not always cruelty, and it is not proof that you were unworthy. It is still a poor way to end a connection, and it leaves others without closure.", ""],
  ["GHOSTING", "C", "Ending all contact with someone suddenly and without explanation, often to avoid conflict or to punish.", "The person on the receiving end can feel anxious and off balance. Each unanswered message can bring hope, then confusion, then self-doubt.", "Messages read but never answered, plans dropped without a word, and blocking or hiding from a person in a shared group or circle.", "Stepping back from a relationship can be healthy when you are unsafe or overwhelmed. Ghosting becomes harmful when it is used to punish, control or dodge honest talk.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["GLOSSOPHOBIA", "P", "A strong fear of speaking aloud, especially in front of others, such as in class or at work.", "Speaking can bring a dry mouth, a pounding heart and a sense that everyone is watching. Words may feel stuck, and the wish to disappear can be strong.", "Avoiding meetings or presentations, rehearsing sentences silently, or speaking in a very quiet voice and cutting answers short.", "Nerves before speaking are common, and many people feel them. It becomes a problem when it stops you from work, school or important conversations.", ""],
  ["GLUTTONY", "V", "Eating far more than the body needs, often past the point of comfort, as a habit or way of coping.", "Eating can bring a quick sense of comfort or reward. Afterward there may be heaviness, regret and a pull to start again.", "Eating quickly, going back for more even when full, and eating to ease stress or boredom rather than hunger.", "Enjoying food is not gluttony. It becomes a harmful habit when eating regularly leads to pain, weight gain, or hiding how much you eat.", ""],
  ["GOALPOST MOVING", "C", "Changing the standard of proof or success after someone has already met it, so they can never win.", "Each time you meet the bar, the bar moves. You may feel tired, discouraged and unsure whether your effort counts.", "Phrases like 'that does not count' or 'that is not what I meant' after you have done what was asked. The conditions keep changing.", "Asking for more information is fair. It is harmful when new demands are added after each success, so that no effort is ever enough.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["GOOSEBUMPS (PILOERECTION)", "B", "Small raised bumps on the skin from tiny muscles at each hair tightening, often from cold or strong emotion.", "Goosebumps can come with a chill, a shiver or a sudden tingle. They are often linked to strong emotion, such as awe, fear or being moved.", "Raised skin on the arms, neck or scalp, sometimes with a shiver. It may happen during music, a tense scene, or a cold room.", "Goosebumps are a normal body reflex, not a sign of illness or weakness. They become a concern only when they come with pain, fever or other changes.", ""],
  ["GOREPHILE", "F", "A pull toward graphic images or stories of gore, sometimes linked to sexual arousal in some people.", "Gore can feel strangely gripping, and some people feel a mix of fascination and unease. Shock may give a rush of focus or excitement.", "Seeking out violent or bloody films, images or stories often, or lingering on injury and gore in ways that crowd out other interests.", "Being curious about gore is not the same as wanting harm. Acting on any sexual interest without consent, or causing real harm to others, is harmful.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["GOURMAND SYNDROME", "B", "A rare change in food interest after a brain injury or stroke, leading to a strong focus on rich, fine food.", "The pull toward rich food can feel intense and hard to resist. Eating may bring pleasure, but also worry about weight or health.", "A sudden strong interest in gourmet meals, eating more than usual, or planning food around every outing after a brain event.", "This is a real medical condition, not a lack of self-control or a moral failing. It is rare, and it needs a doctor to check the cause.", "Because this is rare and linked to brain changes, see a doctor soon for a full check. If it is affecting your mood or safety, call or text 988 in the US. In an emergency, call 911."],
  ["GRACEFUL FORGIVENESS", "U", "Letting go of resentment toward others or yourself in a calm, dignified way, without pretending harm did not happen.", "Forgiveness can bring a lighter chest and less tension when old hurts come up. It may come slowly, in waves, rather than all at once.", "Speaking about a hurt without bitterness, choosing not to repeat old grudges, and treating yourself kindly after a mistake.", "Forgiveness is not the same as excusing harm or returning to an unsafe relationship. You can forgive and still set boundaries.", ""],
  ["GRACEFUL LIFE FLOW", "U", "Moving through life's changes with ease, adjusting to what comes instead of fighting every turn.", "A sense of ease can come when you stop forcing outcomes. Tension eases, and there is more room to notice what is good in the moment.", "Adapting when plans shift, accepting a delay without drama, and choosing the next helpful step rather than arguing with what happened.", "Going with the flow does not mean giving up on your goals or letting others decide everything. It means responding with care instead of constant resistance.", ""],
  ["GRACEFUL RESILIENCE", "U", "Bouncing back from hard times with steadiness and dignity, while still allowing yourself to feel the pain.", "Resilience can feel quiet and steady, like a deep breath after a storm. Some days it feels fragile, even when you are doing well.", "Going back to daily routines after a setback, asking for help when needed, and speaking about hard things without freezing.", "Resilience does not mean never struggling or hiding distress. It is built over time with rest, support and honest feelings, not by pushing through alone.", ""],
  ["GRACEFUL SELF-FORGIVENESS", "U", "Treating your own mistakes with honesty and kindness, and taking the next step instead of staying stuck in shame.", "Self-forgiveness can soften harsh inner talk. It may still feel uncomfortable, because part of you wants to keep punishing yourself.", "Owning a mistake in plain words, apologizing where it fits, and speaking to yourself the way you would speak to a close friend.", "Self-forgiveness is not the same as ignoring harm or avoiding responsibility. It means learning from what happened and still treating yourself with respect.", ""],
  ["GRACEFUL SURRENDER", "U", "Accepting what you cannot control, and releasing the tight grip on outcomes that are not yours to decide.", "Surrender can bring relief, as if a heavy load has been set down. It can also feel frightening at first, like loosening a rope you have held for years.", "Saying 'I cannot change this right now' and mean it, stepping back from a fight you cannot win, and letting others carry their own choices.", "Surrender is not giving up on your values or accepting harm. It is letting go of what is outside your reach, while still acting on what you can change.", ""],
  ["GRACEFUL TRANSFORMATION", "U", "Growing and changing over time in a gentle, steady way, with openness to becoming someone new.", "Change can bring hope and a sense of new possibility. It can also bring grief for the old self and uncertainty about what comes next.", "Trying a new habit, admitting when a belief no longer fits, and letting go of an old role with honesty instead of force.", "Transformation does not happen overnight, and it does not erase your past. It often comes in small shifts, and some days may feel like going backward.", ""],
  ["GRADUAL ESCALATION", "C", "Slowly increasing pressure, demands or boundary-pushing in small steps, so each change seems minor.", "Each step seems small, so it is hard to say when things went too far. You may feel trapped, confused, and unsure whether to speak up.", "Small requests that become larger over time, jokes that grow sharper, or rules added piece by piece until a line has been crossed.", "Gradual change is part of life, and not every shift is a tactic. It is harmful when small steps are used to wear down your limits.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["GRANDIOSE", "R", "Having an inflated sense of one's own importance, talent or status, often beyond what others see.", "Being around someone grandiose can feel draining. Conversations may turn to their achievements, and your own ideas may feel dismissed or overlooked.", "Talking about one's own greatness without pause, expecting special treatment, and dismissing others' input as beneath notice.", "Confidence is healthy, and some people are gifted. It becomes a problem when it is built on needing admiration and leaves little room for others.", ""],
  ["GRANDIOSE NARCISSIST", "R", "A person with a strong need for admiration and a grand view of themselves, with little room for others' needs.", "Being near someone like this can feel like a constant performance. You may feel unseen, criticized for small things, or pressed to keep them looking good.", "Frequent talk of their own success, quick anger at criticism, and treating others mainly as an audience or a tool.", "Pride and healthy self-confidence are not the same as this pattern. Only a trained professional can assess personality, and the pattern is harmful when it hurts others.", ""],
  ["GRANDIOSITY", "R", "An inflated belief in one's own importance, abilities or entitlement, often out of step with reality.", "Being around grandiosity can feel tiring. Others may feel they need to praise constantly or hold back their own ideas.", "Boasting, claims of special talent without evidence, and surprise or anger when others do not see their worth in the same way.", "Feeling proud of your work is healthy. Grandiosity is different: it is an exaggerated sense of self that often hides fragile self-esteem and harms relationships.", ""],
  ["GRANDSTANDER", "R", "A person who seeks attention by performing loudly in public, often to look impressive rather than to help.", "Being near a grandstander can make you feel overshadowed. Their show may take up the room, and honest voices are easily drowned out.", "Speeches aimed at the audience, taking credit for group work, and turning every meeting into a chance to be seen.", "Speaking up and sharing credit are both healthy. It becomes a problem when the focus is on looking impressive, and the work or people are left behind.", ""],
  ["GRAPHOMANIAC", "F", "An intense, compulsive urge to write, sometimes filling pages and notebooks far beyond what the moment needs.", "The urge to write can feel like a pressing itch that needs to be scratched. Stopping may bring restlessness, and writing can bring focus and relief.", "Writing at all hours, filling many notebooks, or drafting long messages that are never sent, sometimes at the cost of sleep or meals.", "Loving to write is not a problem in itself, and many writers work this way. It becomes concerning when writing crowds out rest, work and relationships.", ""],
  ["GRATEFUL", "U", "Feeling and noticing thankfulness for people, moments and things that bring good into your life.", "Gratitude can bring a warm, settled feeling and a sense of being supported. It often grows quietly, and can ease the pull of worry.", "Saying thank you with specific details, noticing small kindnesses, and naming good things at the end of the day.", "Gratitude does not mean ignoring hard feelings or pretending things are fine. You can feel thankful and still feel angry, sad or tired.", ""],
  ["GRATITUDE MICRO-PRACTICE", "K", "A very short daily habit of noticing one good thing and holding it for a moment, taking less than a minute.", "A brief pause for gratitude can feel calming and grounding. Some days it may feel forced at first, then more natural with practice.", "A quick pause at a meal, a bus stop or a bedtime, with a mental note of one thing that went well or was kind.", "The practice is not a way to skip hard feelings or force cheerfulness. It works best as a gentle noticing, not a test of how thankful you should feel.", ""],
  ["GREED", "V", "A strong, ongoing desire for more money, goods or status than is needed, often at the expense of others.", "Greed can bring a quick buzz of wanting, then a restless sense that enough is never quite reached. Satisfaction tends to fade fast.", "Hoarding resources, pushing hard for every advantage, and taking more than a fair share even when others go without.", "Wanting security or wanting to do well is not greed. It becomes a harmful habit when the drive for more hurts others or leaves you unable to feel content.", ""],
  ["GRIEF", "M", "The deep sorrow that follows a loss, such as a death, a breakup, a move or a change you did not choose.", "Grief can arrive in waves, sometimes sharp and sometimes dull. It can bring tears, tiredness, and a sense that the world has shifted.", "Crying at unexpected moments, losing interest in things you enjoy, and sleeping or eating differently for a while.", "Grief is not a problem to be fixed quickly, and there is no correct timeline. It becomes a concern when it stays severe for months or leaves you unable to function.", ""],
  ["GRIEF FRAGMENTATION", "M", "A scattered, broken-up feeling of grief, where sadness comes in pieces and is hard to make sense of.", "Feelings may shift quickly from numb to angry to sad, with little warning. It can feel as if you cannot hold onto one clear thought about the loss.", "Jumping between topics, forgetting tasks, and having sudden waves of emotion that seem unrelated to what is happening now.", "Scattered feelings are a common part of grief, not proof that something is wrong with you. They become a concern when you cannot manage daily tasks or stay safe.", ""],
  ["GRIEF INTEGRATION", "M", "The slow process of making a loss part of your life story, so it no longer takes over every day.", "Integration can bring a sense of meaning and steadiness. The loss stays important, but it no longer feels like the only thing that matters.", "Talking about the loss without being overwhelmed, keeping memories alive, and moving back into work and friendships over time.", "Integrating grief does not mean forgetting or moving on quickly. It is a gradual change, and it can include sadness, memories and new joy together.", ""],
  ["GRIMACING", "B", "A tight, twisted facial expression that can show pain, disgust or strain, or happen without intent.", "A grimace can tighten the jaw and forehead and make the face feel stiff. It may come with discomfort, or simply as a reflex to something unpleasant.", "A scrunched face, lips pulled back, or eyes narrowed in a pained or unpleasant way, sometimes repeated without meaning to.", "A grimace is usually a normal response to pain, taste or stress. It becomes a concern when it happens often without a clear cause, or comes with muscle jerks or trouble moving.", "Repeated grimacing without a clear cause should be checked by a doctor. If it is affecting your mood or safety, call or text 988 in the US. In an emergency, call 911."],
  ["GROTESQUOPHILE", "F", "A pull toward the strange, twisted or deformed, in images, stories or real life, often for a jolt of fascination.", "The grotesque can feel strangely magnetic, with a mix of disgust and interest. Some people feel uneasy afterward, as if the image stays with them.", "Seeking out shocking images, lingering on unusual bodies or injuries, and collecting disturbing material in ways that crowd out other interests.", "Curiosity about odd or unusual things is common and not harmful in itself. It becomes a concern when it leads to harm, distress, or treating real people as objects.", ""],
  ["GROUNDING", "K", "A simple way to bring attention back to the present by noticing your body, breath and surroundings.", "Grounding can bring a sense of solid ground when thoughts feel fast or foggy. You may notice the chair, the floor or your breath more clearly.", "Looking slowly around the room, naming colors or sounds, and placing your feet flat on the floor while you breathe.", "Grounding is not a cure for distress, and it does not mean ignoring what you feel. It is a short tool to help you feel steady enough to take the next step.", ""],
  ["GROUNDING TECHNIQUES", "K", "A set of simple methods that bring attention back to the present, such as touch, temperature and sensory focus.", "Grounding methods can help when feelings feel overwhelming or unreal. Using the senses can make the moment feel more solid and less frightening.", "Holding a cool object, pressing hands together, sipping cold water, or naming textures in the room to shift attention out of the mind.", "Grounding does not erase what you feel, and it is not only for crises. It works best when practiced when calm, so it is ready when you need it.", ""],
  ["GUARDIAN", "U", "A person who protects and looks after others with care, steadiness and a sense of responsibility.", "Being a guardian can bring a deep sense of purpose and warmth. It can also bring weight, worry and the pressure to be always on duty.", "Checking in on family or friends, stepping in during trouble, and making sure others have what they need, without seeking praise.", "Caring for others is a strength, and it is not the same as controlling them. Guarding someone is healthy when it respects their choices and your own limits.", ""],
  ["GUIDANCE", "U", "The quality of offering direction, support or wise advice to others without taking over their choices.", "Giving guidance can feel meaningful and grounding. Receiving it can feel steadying, especially when the advice is honest and kind.", "Listening first, asking questions, sharing a clear suggestion, and letting the other person decide what to do next.", "Guidance is not the same as giving orders or deciding for someone else. It is harmful when advice becomes pressure, and healthy when it respects the other person's choices.", ""],
  ["GUILT", "M", "A feeling of regret or responsibility after doing, or failing to do, something that goes against your values.", "Guilt can bring a heavy stomach, restless thoughts and a wish to make things right. It can be useful, or it can keep looping without an end.", "Apologizing repeatedly, avoiding the person you hurt, or replaying the moment many times and punishing yourself afterward.", "Guilt is not proof that you are a bad person. It is most useful when it leads to repair, and it becomes harmful when it grows into constant self-blame.", ""],
  ["GUILT OVERLOAD", "M", "A state of being weighed down by too much guilt at once, from many duties, mistakes or other people's needs.", "Guilt overload can feel like a heavy pile that never gets smaller. Even small tasks can bring a sense of failing someone.", "Saying yes to everything, apologizing for things outside your control, and feeling exhausted when you rest or say no.", "Feeling guilty about many things is common when life is busy. It becomes a concern when guilt drives your choices, sleep and mood for long periods.", ""],
  ["GUILT TRIPPER", "R", "A person who uses guilt to steer others, often by making them feel responsible for the person's feelings.", "Being around a guilt tripper can leave you feeling responsible for their moods. You may give in to keep the peace, then feel drained and uneasy.", "Sighing, saying 'after all I have done for you', or hinting at hurt, so that a reasonable request turns into a debt you owe.", "Expressing hurt is part of honest relationships. The pattern is harmful when guilt is used to win arguments or control choices, rather than to share real feelings.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["GUILT TRIPPING", "C", "Using guilt, blame or hurt feelings to push someone into doing what the other person wants.", "The person on the receiving end can feel pulled between their own needs and the guilt. Saying no may bring waves of shame and second-guessing.", "Phrases like 'I guess I am just not important to you' or 'after everything I gave up', and sulking until you give in.", "Sharing hurt feelings is part of closeness. It is harmful when guilt is used as a tool to control decisions or to avoid accepting a clear no.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["GUILTY", "M", "The feeling of having done wrong, or believing you have, often with a heavy sense of responsibility.", "Feeling guilty can bring tension, a sinking stomach and restless thinking. It may come even when the harm was small or not really yours.", "Quiet withdrawal, over-apologizing, and extra effort to please others after a mistake or a disagreement.", "Feeling guilty does not always mean you did something wrong. It helps to check the facts and separate real responsibility from fear or other people's blame.", ""],
  ["GUT-TORMENTED", "A", "A state of ongoing distress that shows up in the stomach, such as cramps, churning or nausea from worry.", "The stomach can feel tight, knotted, or unsettled for hours. Worry may seem to live in the gut, making it hard to eat or relax.", "Frequent stomach pain or nausea around stressful events, reaching for the bathroom often, or skipping meals when anxious.", "Stomach symptoms from stress are real and common, and they are not made up. Ongoing pain, weight loss or blood in the stool needs a doctor, not only calming tools.", ""],
  ["HALLUCINATOR", "R", "A person who sees, hears or feels things that others do not, often from illness, stress or other causes.", "Living with hallucinations can be frightening and confusing, both for the person and for people nearby. The experience can feel very real to them.", "Talking to unseen people, reacting to sounds no one else hears, or describing sensations that others cannot notice.", "Hallucinations are not the same as lying or deliberate attention seeking. They can come from many causes, including illness, medication, sleep loss or trauma, and need care.", ""],
  ["HAPHEPHOBE", "P", "A strong fear of being touched by others, even by people one trusts, which can cause distress and avoidance.", "Being touched can bring a sudden jolt of panic or a need to pull away. The body may tense up, and the feeling can linger long after the contact ends.", "Stiffening at a hug, avoiding handshakes, or moving away quickly when someone reaches out, even in a friendly way.", "Not wanting touch is a valid boundary, and it is not rudeness. It becomes a problem when the fear stops you from closeness you want, or from basic care such as medical checks.", ""],
];
